Literal input prompt regression tests
=====================================

  ./maxima-local --no-init --lisp=sbcl \
    --batch-string='run_testsuite(tests=[rtest_literal_prompt,rtest_parallel_input,rtest_ask1]);'

Run each configured Lisp. Require the expected test count and "No unexpected
errors" in the log; a zero process status alone is insufficient. The new file
is also appended to the complete suite.

The 11 examples cover default formatting, empty prompts, literal tildes,
Unicode, numbers, symbols, shared list fragments, prompt markers, return
values and printer-setting restoration. Reading the following answer proves
that formatting consumed no extra input. End-of-file and malformed fragment
lists exercise error handling. Actual worker jobs and the serial fallback
must refuse a question before any callback, output or input consumption.

Custom callbacks receive the original control string and original fragment
list under the caller's printer settings. A callback can delegate to the
default formatter, which retains its decimal and Maxima-specific ~A output.

A private fixed generator checks 128 varied fragment lists against decimal
integer/string references, with signed integers up to 39 digits. A 2048-part
prompt checks that the implementation does not require an argument per
fragment in a single function call.

This supports RETRIEVE's exact flat literal pattern, "~{~A~}". It does not
extend the general AFORMAT grammar or alter mathematical display controls.
